## Findings
1. blocking — scripts/agent-workspace.sh:192 `guard_inputs` invokes `cgit` before validating `PATH`; because `cgit` resolves `git` through that unvalidated path, a rejected relative or agent-controlled entry can execute a session-writable `git` before the guard reports the fault, violating the fail-closed boundary at brief:64. — check: packages/cli/src/w362.test.ts (`W-362-b3` should place a logging `git` in each rejected PATH entry and prove it never runs) [severity: S1] [class: interface×validation-order-masks-errors]
2. blocking — scripts/agent-workspace.sh:287 `readlink -f` synthesizes a canonical path when the final `GUARD_BIN` component is absent. If that valid absent path is under an unrelated linked worktree’s ignored directory, relinking creates only an ignored entry and `old_guard` can delete the worktree although no pre-run link led into it, contrary to brief:71. — check: packages/cli/src/w379.test.ts (`W-379-b3` with absent `GUARD_BIN` under another worktree’s ignored directory) [severity: S1] [class: tests×security-boundary-cases-uncovered]
3. blocking — scripts/agent-workspace.sh:168 `cgit` retains `HOME` without overriding `core.excludesFile`, so Git still reads the default `$HOME/.config/git/ignore`; `worktree remove` can therefore treat a globally ignored untracked file as clean and delete it, despite brief:32 explicitly requiring a file ignored only by the Patron’s global excludes to refuse removal. — check: packages/cli/src/w379.test.ts (`W-379-b3` with an untracked file matched only by `$HOME/.config/git/ignore`) [severity: S1] [class: tests×security-boundary-cases-uncovered]

## Verdict
The guard can execute agent-controlled code before rejecting its inputs and can remove linked worktrees without the required identity or cleanliness proof. These security-sensitive behaviors require another round.
